.\" NOTE: this page contains Cyrillic, and it is the example that matters -- read it
.\" with `nroff -Kutf8 -man', not plain `nroff -man', which renders each byte on its
.\" own.  It is the first page in this tree to need that; cmd/README.md SS10 says so.
.TH WC 1
.SH NAME
wc \- word count
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wc
[
.B \-lwc
]
[ name ... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Wc
counts lines, words and characters in the named files,
or in the standard input if no name appears.
A word is a maximal string of characters
delimited by spaces, tabs or newlines.
.PP
If the optional argument is present,
just the specified counts (lines, words or characters)
are selected by the letters
.BR l ,
.BR w ,
or
.BR c .
.PP
.B Note:
the counts always come out in the order
.BR l ,
.BR w ,
.BR c ,
whatever order the letters were given in, and a bare
.B \-
selects none of them \-
.I wc
then reads its input and prints an empty line.
Both are v7's.
.SH "WORDS, CHARACTERS AND EIGHT BITS"
.B Note:
a
.B character
here is a
.BR byte .
This machine's text is UTF\-8, so a Cyrillic letter is two bytes and counts as two.
.PP
.B Note:
a byte above
.B 0177
is a
.B word constituent,
not a delimiter; only space, tab and newline end a word.
v7's test was
.B "\` \' < c && c < 0177"
\- printable ASCII and nothing else \- which on this machine would have made
.B "\`привет мир\'"
zero words rather than two, every byte of it being 0320 or over.
The test is
.B "c > \` \' && c != 0177"
here.
.PP
The DEL exclusion is v7's and is kept, as is what follows from it: a control character other
than tab and newline neither ends a word nor begins one, so a word with one embedded in it
still counts as one word.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A file that cannot be opened is reported on the diagnostic output and skipped; the remaining
arguments are still counted, and a
.B total
line still appears.
.PP
.B Note:
.I wc
then exits
.BR 1 .
v7 exited 0 however many files it had failed to open, so no shell script could tell a run
that counted everything from one that counted half of it.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sum(1)
